WHAT: WK_VPIANO_SELFTEST=1 makes the app narrate itself on stdout -- platform,
      style, font-family count, screen size, then a repeating STATE line (how
      many top-levels are visible, which MIDI backends the static-plugin scan
      found) and a KEYBD line with the piano widget's rect in surface
      coordinates -- plus a SENT/RECV line for every MIDI note that leaves or
      arrives. Also lets WK_VPIANO_MIDI_THRU override the persisted MIDI-thru
      setting.
WHY:  TEST SCAFFOLDING, and the only kind that catches what matters here. A
      headless harness can read pixels, but a pixel histogram proves "not
      blank" -- it cannot tell a piano that is wired to a synth from one that
      is wired to nothing, which is the entire claim of this port.
        * SENT is emitted where the app hands a note to its MIDI output, so a
          harness can click a real key with a real wasi:surface pointer event
          and then require the SAME note number and velocity to appear in the
          terminal of a DIFFERENT node -- the fluidsynth synth on the other end
          of a canvas wire. Tying the two logs together by value is what makes
          it a test of the fabric rather than of two coincidences.
        * RECV is the return path: MIDI injected into this node's inbox has to
          come out of drumstick's parser as a decoded note and light the key up.
        * STATE names the backends. If BackendManager had found nothing,
          VPiano::initialize() would already have qFatal'd -- so the line
          existing is half the claim, and naming them stops a WRONG backend
          from passing.
        * KEYBD is published on a repeating timer, not once: the wk QPA forces
          the first top-level to the full surface a few frames in, so a rect
          read at singleShot(0) is the pre-resize geometry and aiming at it
          misses. The repeat doubles as the only evidence that QTimer deadlines
          survive being folded into the frame wait -- the exact mechanism the
          MIDI input pump depends on.
        * WK_VPIANO_MIDI_THRU exists because thru is normally a checkbox in a
          dialog persisted to QSettings, and a node started from a .wk file has
          neither a person at the dialog nor somewhere to persist. It is what
          makes a USB keyboard play THROUGH the app to a synth while lighting
          its keys.
      The reporter deliberately reads QPluginLoader::staticInstances() rather
      than constructing a second BackendManager: that constructor calls
      refresh(), which rewrites the public name and excluded-connection list of
      the very singletons being reported.
UPSTREAM: no. Ours, for testing, and every line of it is behind `#ifdef
      __wasi__` / `#if defined(WK_BACKEND)`, so it does not exist in a desktop
      build. Upstream would rightly want this as a QTest, which cannot run here
      (FEATURE_testlib=OFF, and a wasip2 component has no test harness).
